  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 431 AM MST Fri Nov 6 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nAs a mild trough moves to the east, weak ridging aloft builds to \nthe west today, eventually encapturing Montana by the evening. \nThis will allow temperatures to be seasonally warm and skies to \nremain mostly sunny. Breezy conditions will develop over \nCentral/North-Central Montana, with higher winds occurring along \nthe Northern Rocky Mountain Front. 700 mb winds increase in the \nafternoon to around 60- 70 kts, which due to peak diurnal heating \nand terrain-induced turbulent mixing, are expected to mix down to \nthe surface. This will result in winds gusting up to 65 mph along \nthe Northern Rocky Mountain Front, prompting the continuation of \ntoday's High Wind Watch. However, sounding and cross section \nanalysis support the stronger winds mixing down to the surface \nlater in the afternoon, hence the watch start time being pushed \nback to 4 PM. The strongest winds are expected late this evening.\n\nEntering the weekend, the ridging aloft continues to build, \nallowing lower elevation high temperatures to climb into the upper\n50s to mid 60s. Winds will be milder Saturday afternoon, as the \nstronger winds aloft move much higher in the atmosphere and do not\nhave the opportunity to mix down to the surface. The break in the\nwinds is short lived, as upper level troughing moves north of the\nIdaho Panhandle on Sunday, tightening the pressure gradient. \nStrong 700 mb winds, around 70-80 kts, will once again mix down to\nthe surface across the Northern Rocky Mountain Front, prompting \nthe continuation of Sunday's High Wind Watch. While the strongest \nwinds will be confined to the Northern Rocky Mountain Front, \ngenerally windy conditions will be more widespread across the CWA \ndue to the tightening pressure gradient. \n\nMonday morning brings a brief period of zonal flow aloft as the \nupper level troughing moves to the east. This is followed by a \ngeneral pattern of building ridging with embedded shortwaves \nthroughout next week, creating on-and-off increased precipitation \nchances over the higher elevations of the western portion of the \nCWA. Models are showing signs of a more potent upper level trough \ndigging south through Central MT mid next week, indicating the \npotential of a return of stronger winds. -Dzomba\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nAs of writing this AFD (~3 AM), the high wind watch was kept as a\nwatch and not upgraded to a warning. This is because of \nuncertainty remaining in how strong the winds will be. Model \nguidance shows confidence in surface winds gusting up to 65 mph. \nHowever, there remains a 50-60% chance of winds gusting greater \nthan 75 mph; enough to keep the watch, but not quite enough to \nupgrade to a warning. \n\nThere is more confidence in the strongest winds occurring on \nSunday. BUFKIT analysis indicates increasing, unidirectional cross\nbarrier flow, which is likely to support momentum transfer to the\nsurface solely through diurnal mixing processes. Additionally, \nwithin BUFKIT exists ideal thermal profiles to support mountain \nwave activity, which combined with a moderately strong surface \npressure gradient will further enhance winds speeds during the day\non Sunday. - Moldan/Dzomba",
